  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output A.1. More accurate and sustainable statistics on decent work using the latest statistical standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Knowledge base on Decent Work in the country was extended through new statistical labour surveys and expanded and strengthened information systems on national and subnational labour markets. 
The Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics (IBGE), implemented a pioneer national household survey specifically on COVID-19 (applied between May/November 2020). The survey identified the impacts of the pandemic on the labour market and health. The survey results were important for planning immediate response actions to the crisis in the area of social protection and resumption of employment, work, and income generation. 
IBGE also conducted a supplement component of the Continuous Household Survey (PNAD-C) on Information and Communication Technology (ICT). Specific questions that deepened the knowledge about ICT use by the workforce were included. This allows the generation of inputs for digital inclusion in the context of the pandemic and the resumption of economic activities. 
In the scope of the Smartlab Platform for Decent Work, the content of the Observatory of Brazilian Municipalities was expanded, emphasizing the &quot;thermometer&quot; (visual graphics) of the formal labour market, which presents the availability of vacancies every quarter for country: 27 states and 5,570 municipalities. The ILO Office in Brazil technically supported the Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics (IBGE), in the design and implementation of the pioneer national household COVID19 survey (applied between May/November 2020),. In addition to ensuring the implementation of the statistical recommendations for data collection during the pandemic, the ILO also proposed information on telecommuting, identification of workers in delivery services and of people who did not look for work due to the pandemic, and access to some emergency assistance related to COVID.
On the PNAD survey, the contribution of the ILO allowed the inclusion of specific questions to the questionnaire that deepened the knowledge about ICT.
Also, the ILO&apos;s participation in the Advisory Commission of the Demographic Census 2022 ensured the maintenance of the essential content of work and income in the questionnaire, which will strengthen the territorial information system on the labour market and the construction of decent indicators at national, state and municipal scale.
The newest revision of the Smartlab Platform for Decent Work is the fruit of a partnership between the Labour Inspectorate, the Labour Prosecutor&apos;s Office and the ILO.</narrative>
   </description>
